    "case_id": "593",
    "case_summary": "A 46-year-old woman with a medical history of ovarian serous carcinoma treated with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomies, with recurrent refractory disease on salvage chemotherapy, presented to the emergency department with dyspnea and cough for 1 day.\n\nOn presentation:- Temperature: Afebrile- Pulse rate: 99 beats/min- Respiratory rate: 21 breaths/min  - Blood pressure: 116/84 mm Hg- SpO₂: 90% on room airLater developed:- Left-sided pleuritic chest pain- Oxygen saturation dropped to 70%- Required non-rebreather mask to maintain O2 saturation of 90%\n\nInitial Tests:- Computed tomography angiography:  Image Title: Initial CT angiography  Image Description: Shows pulmonary embolism in the right pulmonary artery branching into segmental and lobar arteries, left segmental artery embolus, bilateral pleural effusions, evidence of right heart strainPost-procedure Tests:- ECG: T-wave inversions in anterior leads- High-sensitivity troponins: Not elevated- Arterial blood gas:   - pH: 7.46  - PCO₂: 30  - PO₂: 52  - Bicarbonate: 21.3- Chest radiograph: Improvement of pleural effusion, patchy infiltration along left heart border- Repeat CT angiography:  Image Title: Follow-up CT scan  Image Description: Decreased left-sided pleural effusion with near-complete left lower-lobe infiltrates in space previously occupied by pleural fluid- Pleural fluid analysis: Exudate with malignant cells",
